<description>
Paint the top-connected middle channel between two orange path components teal.

Input structure
- The background is black.
- One foreground color forms exactly two 4-connected one-pixel-wide path components.
- Optional non-background control pixels may sit in the channel between the two paths.

Rule
- Dilate each path component by one cell in the 8-neighborhood and intersect the two dilations; this gives the one-cell-wide middle channel.
- A control pixel in that channel is a blocker only when its 4-neighbors touch fewer than both path components; otherwise it is part of the channel and is repainted.
- After removing blockers, paint the channel component connected to the top edge teal and keep all other pixels unchanged.

Key insight
- The desired pixels are not found by region filling; they are the geometric overlap of the two paths' one-cell halos, with weakly attached control pixels splitting the channel.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "middle-channel mask formed by intersecting the 8-neighborhood halos of the two orange 4-connected path objects",
  "transform": "recolor the top-connected passable channel to teal; a non-bg control pixel blocks the channel only if it is not 4-adjacent to both orange path objects, otherwise it is recolored too"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 10x10, black background
  walls:
    color: orange (7), exactly 2 four-connected components
    shape: each is a 1-pixel-wide polyline (max 2 four-neighbors per cell)
    structure: both start at row 0 with columns exactly 2 apart (1-cell channel gap)
    segments: alternating V-H-V-H-... with 2-4 turns (3-5 segments)
    horizontal_segments: same length for both paths, length 2-4, all jog same direction (L or R)
    vertical_segments: length 1-7 per segment; both paths have same H lengths but complementary V lengths
    endpoints: both paths start at row 0; most end at row 9 or a side edge
    path_size: 12-17 cells per component [observed]
  channel:
    definition: intersection of 8-dilation of both wall components, excluding wall cells
    width: always exactly 1 cell wide (max 2 four-neighbors per channel cell)
    shape: mirrors the corridor between the two walls; same turn structure
    size: 12-17 cells; starts at row 0, usually reaches row 9 or a side edge
  blocker:
    color: maroon (9), 0 or 1 pixel, placed inside the channel
    blocking_rule: blocks iff 4-adjacent to only 1 wall component (at a turn cell)
    non-blocking: at a straight section (4-adj to both walls) -> gets overwritten to teal
  output:
    paint: top-connected passable channel component -> teal (8)
    when_no_block: entire channel painted teal (maroon overwritten if present)
    when_blocked: channel splits at blocker; only top-connected portion painted teal; blocker stays maroon

generation_strategy:
  approach: channel-first (output-first variant)
  steps:
    1. Generate a zigzag channel polyline starting at a random column in row 0, going down.
       Alternate V(2-7) and H(2-4) segments, 2-4 turns, all H segments jog same direction (L or R).
       Ensure the polyline stays in bounds (cols 1..8 to leave room for walls).
    2. Build the two wall paths by placing orange cells on both sides of the channel.
       For vertical channel segments, walls are 1 cell left and right.
       For horizontal segments, walls are 1 cell above and below.
       At corners, add the outer-wall elbow cell to maintain connectivity.
    3. Verify both wall components are 4-connected and 1-pixel wide.
    4. Optionally place a maroon blocker in the channel - either at a turn cell (blocking)
       or at a straight cell (non-blocking). ~60% of examples have a blocker [observed].
    5. Derive output: compute passable channel (remove blocking maroon), paint top-connected
       component teal. Non-blocking maroon gets overwritten to teal.
```
</generation_spec>
